      "change": "No change to this commit is needed. When building U2b, keep the confirmation tied to the launched pid, and add a zombie row that leaves runner.json in place so the case stays covered.",
      "claim": "A runner that has just died but has not yet been reaped by its parent (a zombie) counts as gone for the stop and as still running for the launch confirmation. If its runner.json is still there, the repair reports a failed replacement even though the new runner may be starting normally. The step works and recovers on its own: the transition file is removed and the next pass decides afresh. The next unit, U2b, removes the cause, because it confirms the launch only when runner.json names the pid it launched. That is why this should not refuse the commit.",
      "class": "regression",
      "evidence": "Trace at the reviewed tree: continueReplacement uses identity.LiveRef, which reads a zombie as dead (runner_replacement.go:92-99; identity.go:215-221). launchReplacement then calls launchRunner, whose AwaitRunnerStart first asks liveRunner (runner.go, AwaitRunnerStart). liveRunnerWithProber does not check Zombie (runner.go:1673-1694), so it returns the dead runner's record at once. enginepin.Read refuses a zombie (execution.go:35), giving 'replacement runner execution not verified'. Reproduced with a real unreaped child on a throwaway copy; with runner.json removed the same step instead reported the launched child's real exit. runner.json survives only when the runner did not exit in an orderly way, for example at the drain limit. The old stop loop waited on liveRunnerWithProber, so it waited for the reap. The new test's zombie row deletes runner.json first (runner_replacement_test.go, TestAStopTellsGoneFromReusedFromUnknown), so it does not cover this. The fake runner in TestRecoveryReplacesALiveRunnerThroughTheRealRepair leaves its runner.json behind, so that proof could in principle hit the same moment under heavy load until U2b lands (inferred, not observed).",
